                <div>
                  <p><span>16</span>
                  </p>
                </div>
                <p>
                  </p><h2>
                    Pseudo-elements
                  </h2>
                <p></p>
                
              </div>
              <p>
                  
                  Article
                
                
                
                

                
              </p>
              <p>
                
                  A pseudo-element is like adding or targeting an extra element without having to add more HTML. They have a variety of roles and you can learn about them in this module.
                
                </p>
              
            
          </div>
      
        <div type="article" id="article-https://web.dev/learn/css/pseudo-classes" path="https://web.dev/learn/css/pseudo-classes">
            
              <div>
                <div>
                  <p><span>17</span>
                  </p>
                </div>
                <p>
                  </p><h2>
                    Pseudo-classes
                  </h2>
                <p></p>
                
              </div>
              <p>
                  
                  Article
                
                
                
                

                
              </p>
              <p>
                
                  Pseudo-classes let you apply CSS based on state changes. This means that your design can react to user input such as an invalid email address.
                
                </p>
              
            
          </div>
      
        <div type="article" id="article-https://web.dev/learn/css/borders" path="https://web.dev/learn/css/borders">
            
